  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ダイオウマツ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%80%E3%82%A4%E3%82%AA%E3%82%A6%E3%83%9E%E3%83%84",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 107539314,
      "text": "ダイオウマツ とは、マツ科マツ属の樹木である。ダイオウショウともいう。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Longleaf pine」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longleaf_pine",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1378474043,
      "text": "The longleaf pine is a species of conifer in the family Pinaceae, a pine native to the Southeastern United States, found along the coastal plain from East Texas to southern Virginia, extending into northern and central Florida. In this area it is also known as \"yellow pine\" or \"long leaf yellow pine\", although it is properly just one out of a number of species termed yellow pine. It reaches a height of 30–35 m (98–115 ft) and a diameter of 0.7 m (28 in). In the past, before extensive logging, they reportedly grew to 47 m (154 ft) with a diameter of 1.2 m (47 in). The tree is a cultural symbol of the Southern United States, being the official state tree of Alabama. This particular species is one of the eight pine tree species that falls under the \"Pine\" designation as the state tree of North Carolina."
    }
